result:
Fixed in both places that build an abstract `SocketAddr`:

- `tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:82` (`UnixListener::bind`)
- `tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:78` (`UnixStream::connect`)

Both detected the leading NUL with `starts_with(b"\0")` and then passed the *whole* byte string to `StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name`. That constructor prepends the NUL itself, so `"\0aaa"` was registered as the abstract name `"\0aaa"` — one byte longer than requested, and it round-trips out of `as_abstract_name()` as `b"\0aaa"` instead of `b"aaa"`. The change switches to `strip_prefix(b"\0")` so the name is passed without its marker byte:

```rust
if let Some(name) = os_str_bytes.strip_prefix(b"\0") {
    // `from_abstract_name` prepends the leading null byte itself, so
    // it must be given the name without it.
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(name)?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

Verified: `uds_stream` passes all 8 tests including `abstract_socket_name`, and `uds_datagram`/`uds_split`/`uds_cred` are unaffected (14 tests pass). Non-Linux targets are unchanged — they still take the `from_pathname` path.
